A||||15||RobH||Fiero's and the snow....||||5
Z||000000||RobH||10-27-1999||01:24 PM||||I have a '85 Sport Coupe with the standard steel(185/80R13) wheels.  I just aquired a set of the aluminum wheels and I plan on buying a set of 215/60R14 for it.  I live in SC, but will probably be moving to Idaho within the next 4 months.<P>How well does the Fiero handle in the snow?  Any problems since the car is so light, and rear wheel drive?  Anyone recommend some good tires?<P>Thanks in advance,<BR>Rob||||reg||5
Z||000001||ChadMan||10-27-1999||01:41 PM||||I have experience with three Fieros in snow, as follows:<P>84 Coupe - Dunlop Axiom 185/80R13 - great in snow<BR>86 SE V6 - Firestone Firehawk SS 215/60R14 - fair in snow<BR>88 Coupe - Yokohama S405 215/60R14 - lousy in snow||||reg||1
Z||000002||ratt||10-27-1999||04:01 PM||||I drive my Fiero in the winter here in the Kootenays (B.C.), not to far from Idaho.  I suggest getting tires with the deepest lugs you can find for all four corners.  Even with a sandbag in the front, the car does tend to float in heavy slush.  I wouldn't recommend all-seasons, just use winters for winter driving.  I also suggest you use your steel rims for winter, no point in wrecking your aluminum rims.  Overall the car drives well in the snow, just keep in mind the front end is light (and the car is low) & drive accordingly.  If the snow is deep enough it will collect in front of the radiator & you will have to stop & clear it out or you'll overheat.  The only time I've had difficulties is when I encountered freezing rain outside of Spokane Wa. (mid december typ.)  The heater couldn't keep the ice off the windshield.  I had to stop every 15 minutes to scrape it clean.  Both my wife & I have Fiero's and we haven't had any winter driving problems.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-27-1999||04:47 PM||||freezing rain is less of a problem if you load some of that antifreeze washer in the tank.<P>plus keep a spray bottle full of it around some place handy for the winter. Spray the glass lightly before you go in for the night. you may still get ice buildup but it's bond to the glass will be much weaker making for quicker removal.<P>Comes in handy removing frost too... spray on wipe off frost gone.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-27-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||10-27-1999||06:00 PM||||Stay tuned for the next edition of Martha Stewart Orgre's Car Talk.||||reg||1
Z||000005||fierospeeder||10-28-1999||12:58 AM||||get good tires, that is the most important.  Also when its icy out, dont go into turns to fast, cause ive went into a shopping mall lot when it was icy at night, so no one was there, and i did a 360 coming into the parking lot.  Fieros dont have abs brakes so locking up is common, so no tailgating.  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||ChadMan||10-28-1999||08:54 AM||||Speaking of Fieros and winter - has anybody else noticed how tough the suspensions on these little cars seem to be?  Potholes, curbs, parking chocks (don't ask) - nothing seems to be able to knock a Fiero out of alignment.<P>Maybe that's why Fieros weigh so much more than CRX's and MR2's - they're actually built to last through American winters.||||reg||14
Z||000007||fiero56||10-28-1999||09:23 AM||||Here in Centerville a pothole is a very rare ocurrance.  But, when I head back to Columbus for college, the second I hit I-70 it's potholes and ruts for the next 50 miles.  Horrible stuff.  I can't believe my car still steers straight after some of the stuff it's driven through.  I would have to say Columbus roads along with I-70 are some of the worst I've driven. Train tracks, potholes, "inverted potholes"(potholes that after being patched stick up an inch or so above the road), and some of the deepest ruts I've ever seen(can't wait to see what it's like when rain goes in those and freezes).  I never see this stuff back home.  I think most of it is due to the large amount of trucking going through Columbus though.||||reg||1
Z||000008||theogre||10-28-1999||08:40 PM||||I can assure you..... Fiero can be knocked out of alignment.... I hit a crater on a PA road you could park a bus in. a large bus. My next sat was spent at the alignment shop.||||reg||1
Z||000009||lowCG||10-28-1999||09:18 PM||||I highly recommend the Bridgstone Blizzak snowtire,think they have a few different ones,but the original,cheaper ones work excellent.Have a set on my lowered Jetta,get me through roads where the snow is up to the grill.<BR>Once took a wrong turn on a rainy night in my old Fiero,onto some traintracks,didn't want to stop and get stuck between the ties,so I stepped on it,got into second,then I was going fast enough to float on top of the ties,and then met up with some pavement again in a few feet,was only on the tracks for about a  city block.Checked everything out the next day,figured I'd tweaked or blown a shock/strut for sure,but it was all fine,even the alignment.Xgirlfriend thought we were going to die for sure.||||reg||1
Z||000010||kornholio||10-28-1999||09:43 PM||||what kind of all season tires are good in the snow?||||reg||1
Z||000011||turbo86se||10-29-1999||11:52 AM||||Rob, where in SC do you live? I live in Murrells Inlet, you anywhere near? Before I lived in SC I lived in MD, and had plenty of experience with snow. I had a 85 sport coupe, lowered, 5-spd. In the snow I always ran with 13" wheels with Cooper tires, nothing fancy, just standard tires.I had no problems at all getting around in the snow. I believe they are 185/70/13. I still have the wheels and tires in MD, just in case it snows when I visit. ||||reg||1
Z||000012||RobH||10-30-1999||10:59 AM||||turbo86se, I live up near Aiken, looks like a couple hours from Murrells Inlet.  You must be the envy of all your friends who have salt/rust damage to their cars from living by the coast  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Looks like the best thing for me to do is to keep my steel wheels and get some winter tires just for them, and use the aluminum wheels for Spring - Fall driving.<P>Thanks to all who have responded...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||olympic||10-31-1999||12:55 AM||||I live in Saskatchewan, Canada(8 months winter and 4 months of poor skating) Get the Blizzaks, their fairly cheap and are great on even bare ice.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Patrick||10-31-1999||01:05 AM||||<BR>I thought it was ten months of winter and two months of poor sledding. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||14
Z||000015||olympic||10-31-1999||05:19 PM||||That was before the good old green house effect!!!||||reg||12
